---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
    title="About & Contact - Claire Nyquist"
    description="Minneapolis-based data visualization designer, developer, and public health analyst. Specializing in interactive data visualizations using R, Python, JavaScript, and D3. Contact for collaboration opportunities."
>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <!-- Hero Section -->
        <div class="text-center mb-16">
            <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">
                About & Contact
            </h1>
            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 max-w-3xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
                Where Epidemiology Meets Design
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto mb-16">
            <!-- About Section -->
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-12 items-start">
                <!-- Headshot Image -->
                <div class="lg:col-span-1">
                    <div class="relative">
                        <img
                            src="/images/headshot.jpeg"
                            alt="Professional headshot"
                            class="w-full h-auto object-contain rounded-lg shadow-lg"
                        />
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- About Content -->
                <div class="lg:col-span-2">
                    <div
                        class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6"
                    >
                        <p>Hi, I'm Claire.</p>
                        <p>
                            I live at the intersection of <em
                                >How does this virus work?</em
                            > and <em
                                >How can I make this spreadsheet moderately
                                intelligible&mdash;maybe even beautiful?
                            </em>
                            <p></p> With a Master of Public Health from the University
                            of Minnesota and First Class Honors in Graphic Communication
                            Design from the University of Westminster in London, I've
                            spent my career translating complex (and sometimes scary)
                            science into actionable insights.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Most people see a massive spreadsheet of infectious
                            disease data and want to close their laptop. I want
                            to open Adobe Illustrator and Claude Code. I've
                            distributed vaccines for the State of Minnesota,
                            researched PPE barriers for long-term care
                            facilities, and built custom data visualizations in
                            R and Python. My goal is always the same: improving
                            health outcomes for people around the world.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <strong
                                >Why work with a public health data designer?</strong
                            ><br />
                            <br />
                            I've worked in pediatric ERs, veterinary diagnostic labs,
                            and as a federal consultant. From HTML/CSS to JavaScript
                            and R, I build digital experiences that are accessible,
                            functional, and statistically sound. I've also spent my
                            free time volunteering at the Minneapolis Institute of
                            Art and The Bakken Museum. I know how to spark curiosity
                            in people&mdash;whether they are five years old or a federal
                            policymaker.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            I'm an award-winning analyst who believes that
                            public health preparedness is only as good as its
                            communication strategy.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            When I'm not organizing tcplot (our local Twin
                            Cities data-viz community) or debating the universal
                            principles of design, you can find me wandering
                            around Minneapolis over-analyzing the typography on
                            a craft beer can or explaining zoonotic pathogens
                            and malaria vaccines to anyone who will listen.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Want to collaborate on a project that requires both
                            a microscope and a paintbrush? Get in touch at
                            clairenyquist@gmail.com.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</BaseLayout>
